# JUnit4 declaration task: code review round 2

Verdict: **FAIL** for one remaining malformed-import edge. All four round 1
findings are resolved in the reviewed source.

Reviewed module SHA-256:
fb4fdef8c532c2fabde87464dfa71eced00809b45d490746f3d2ccca1fe84d32

## Blocking finding

**P2 — Malformed or Java-only-unsupported alias imports can still confirm
annotations** (android_junit4_declarations.rs, import alias parsing around
lines 908–920; direct binding around lines 1045–1060).

The alias branch accepts a missing alias as None and emits only a file warning;
it does not mark the parsed import header unsupported. Consequently the malformed
Kotlin import below becomes the ordinary unique explicit JUnit4 import again:

~~~kotlin
import org.junit.Test as ;
class Suite { @Test fun actual() {} }
~~~

The same branch also accepts alias syntax for Java. A malformed Java import that
does not shadow org/Test therefore leaves qualified confirmation enabled:

~~~java
import other.Annotation as Check;
class Suite { @org.junit.Test public void actual() {} }
~~~

Require a valid alias identifier after Kotlin as. Mark missing aliases and any
Java alias import unsupported for confirmation, retain their source/import
evidence, and add regression assertions that these methods remain unresolved.

